1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,994
Welcome to lesson twenty-two: GitOps with Argo CD and Flux.

2
00:00:05,094 --> 00:00:15,456
You'll make Git the single source of truth for what runs in your clusters. Deployments become pull requests, rollbacks become git revert, and drift is detected and fixed automatically.

3
00:00:16,806 --> 00:00:25,536
GitOps has four principles. The desired state is declarative, versioned in Git, pulled automatically by agents, and continuously reconciled.

4
00:00:25,636 --> 00:00:34,702
In classic CI and CD, the pipeline pushes changes with cluster credentials, and nobody notices if someone changes the cluster by hand later.

5
00:00:34,802 --> 00:00:42,508
With GitOps, an agent inside the cluster pulls from Git and keeps correcting any difference. The credentials stay inside the cluster.

6
00:00:43,858 --> 00:00:53,820
The benefits are big: a full audit trail, reviews through pull requests, easy rollback, drift detection, better security, and fast disaster recovery.

7
00:00:53,920 --> 00:01:03,322
But GitOps doesn't solve everything alone. You still need a plan for secrets, data backups, ordering, emergencies, and fields that controllers change.

8
00:01:04,672 --> 00:01:08,522
Keep app code and deployment config in separate repositories.

9
00:01:08,622 --> 00:01:16,504
The app repository builds, scans and signs the image, then opens a pull request in the config repository with the new digest.

10
00:01:16,604 --> 00:01:25,606
In the config repository, use one directory per environment on a single branch. Avoid a branch per environment, which leads to drift and constant conflicts.

11
00:01:26,956 --> 00:01:31,014
Argo CD is a popular GitOps tool with a rich web UI.

12
00:01:31,114 --> 00:01:38,756
Its application controller compares the cluster with Git, and its repo server renders manifests from plain YAML, Kustomize or Helm.

13
00:01:38,856 --> 00:01:43,330
Note that it renders Helm charts itself, so they don't appear in helm list.

14
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:51,314
An Application says: take this path in this Git repository, and deploy it to this cluster and namespace.

15
00:01:51,414 --> 00:01:58,928
Automated sync applies Git changes. Prune deletes resources removed from Git. Self heal reverts manual changes in the cluster.

16
00:01:59,028 --> 00:02:07,134
And ignore differences tells Argo CD to leave fields alone that a controller owns, like replicas when an HPA manages scaling.

17
00:02:08,484 --> 00:02:16,654
Argo CD shows two separate statuses. Sync status says whether the cluster matches Git. Health status says whether it actually works.

18
00:02:16,754 --> 00:02:22,012
Synced doesn't mean healthy. A Deployment can match Git perfectly while crash-looping.

19
00:02:22,112 --> 00:02:30,618
For ordering, use sync waves, so CRDs and namespaces come first, and hooks, such as a migration Job that runs before the sync.

20
00:02:31,968 --> 00:02:41,402
AppProjects are guardrails for teams. They limit which repositories, clusters and namespaces a team can use, and what kinds of resources they can create.

21
00:02:41,502 --> 00:02:51,160
ApplicationSets generate many Applications from one template: one per environment, one per cluster, one per folder, or even one per open pull request.

22
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:57,318
Because Argo CD can deploy anything, protect the config repository just like production itself.

23
00:02:58,668 --> 00:03:05,622
Flux is the other major GitOps tool. It's a set of focused controllers configured entirely with custom resources.

24
00:03:05,722 --> 00:03:16,532
A GitRepository fetches the source, and Flux's Kustomization object applies it, with pruning, health checks and built-in SOPS decryption. HelmRelease creates real Helm releases.

25
00:03:16,632 --> 00:03:22,274
flux bootstrap installs Flux and commits its own manifests, so Flux manages itself.

26
00:03:23,624 --> 00:03:32,162
Both tools are excellent. Argo CD has a strong UI and central management of many clusters. Flux is lightweight and fully Kubernetes-native.

27
00:03:32,262 --> 00:03:44,464
For safe releases, add progressive delivery. Argo Rollouts shifts traffic step by step, checks metrics from Prometheus, and automatically rolls back if errors rise. Flagger does the same for Flux.

28
00:03:45,814 --> 00:03:51,520
Never commit plain Secrets. The External Secrets Operator keeps values out of Git entirely.

29
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:57,918
Make Git the only way to change production, with read-only access for people and a documented emergency procedure.

30
00:03:58,018 --> 00:04:05,628
Turn on self heal and prune, validate changes in CI, deploy by digest, and practice rebuilding a cluster from Git.

31
00:04:06,978 --> 00:04:11,740
In the lab, you'll install Argo CD and deploy your first Application.

32
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:21,130
You'll watch self heal revert a manual change, generate dev and prod from one ApplicationSet, and fix a fight between an HPA and Argo CD.

33
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:25,496
With a GitHub fork, you'll deploy by commit and roll back by revert.

34
00:04:26,846 --> 00:04:27,944
Let's recap.

35
00:04:28,044 --> 00:04:34,006
With GitOps, the desired state lives in Git and an in-cluster agent keeps the cluster matching it.

36
00:04:34,106 --> 00:04:41,604
Use a config repository with directories per environment, remember that synced isn't healthy, and keep secrets out of Git.

37
00:04:41,704 --> 00:04:46,338
Next, you'll extend Kubernetes with your own resources and controllers.
